{
  "dom_id": "step-q336655",
  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "A series RL circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $8\\,\\text{V},$ a resistance of $20\\,Ω,$ and an inductance of $4\\,\\text{H}.$\nFind, rounded to two decimal places, the circuit's current after $0.02$ seconds given that the initial current is $2\\,\\text{A}.$\n\nThe current after $0.02$ seconds is\n{{field-1}} amperes.",
  "worked_solution": "Kirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=20\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=20I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=4\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=4\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n8=20I+4\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+5I=2.\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+5{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-5t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${I}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0,$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+5\\alpha =2\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{2}{5}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{2}{5}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-5t}+\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial current of $2\\,\\text{A}.$ So, we can apply the initial condition $I(0)=2,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(0) & =2 \\\\ A{e}^{-5(0)}+\\frac{2}{5} & =2 \\\\ A & =\\frac{8}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $I(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & =\\frac{8}{5}{e}^{-5t}+\\frac{2}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}(4{e}^{-5t}+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the current in the circuit after $t=0.02$ seconds is\n\n$$\nI(0.02)=\\frac{2}{5}(4{e}^{-5(0.02)}+1)≈1.85\\,\\text{A},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
